{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 28, :comments ["<b>And Elohim blessed them, and Elohim said to them: Be fruitful and multiply.</b> Had this been a single blessing — namely, the blessing of reproduction that he then proceeds to mention — it would have said: ויברך אותם אלהים לאמר פרו ורבו, as it says concerning the fish. But because it returned to say ויאמר אלהים, it must be explained that this blessing is one thing in itself, and the blessing of reproduction is another blessing. And the Holy One, blessed be He, mentioned the Name with each one, to indicate that there are two blessings. The first blessing is that He blessed the transformative power that is in the human body, so that man would live forever, body and soul, and that his food and nourishment would be blessed, filling the deficiency that departs from his body each day, so that neither weakness nor old age would overtake him. And so the masters of medicine have written in their books, that man in this world needs to restore to his body and attach to it something that will lubricate it and moisten it, and that will restore to it the moisture that has been lost from it for two reasons: one, because of the natural heat that is within the body; and second, because of the surrounding air on the outside, which dries its moisture and dampness. And that food which he eats and attaches to his body must be of the nature of the moisture that is lacking from each and every limb. But the replacements that are produced in the body from food and drink do not fill all of this deficiency, and they are not identical to the substance lacking in every respect; rather, they contain dregs and waste matter, and they fall short of the form of the limb a little. And this is the cause of illness, of the weakening of strength, and of the wasting of flesh in the days of old age. For if the food were to fill all the deficiency, and if man were able to restore it to his body in the same measure and form as the moisture and dampness that departs from him — no less and no more — he would never come to weakness or old age, nor would man ever die. And concerning this blessing it says: ויברך אותם אלהים — He blessed them with the blessing of Elohim, that is, that they would endure thereby eternally like Elohim, in the manner of what is written (Psalms 82:6): ״אני אמרתי אלהים אתם״." "<b>And thus</b> wrote the sage, author of the book Ha-Megillah: that this blessing is the one given to the first man — that his food would be from the fruit of the earth and its produce, filling for him the deficiency that departs from his body in every respect, in its measure and form; and accordingly it would have been fitting for him to live forever, for He gave him the transformative power in its full potency." "<b>And this is</b> what is written: ״הנה נתתי לכם את כל עשב זורע זרע״. The produce attached to the ground, which is food for human beings, Scripture calls עשב זורע זרע; and the herbage that is food for beasts it calls חציר and ירק דשא עשב. And here it mentioned זורע זרע and not מזריע זרע, as it mentioned at their creation on the third day: ״תדשא הארץ דשא עשב מזריע זרע״. For the herbage produces seed and scatters it, but it does not itself turn back into seed. Because of this, here it mentioned זורע זרע — like a man who sows seed in order to acquire another seed like it; and it comes to say that the Holy One, blessed be He, placed in his nourishment the transformative power in its perfection, so that it would replace and renew in his body what was lacking from it — like it and in its form and in its measure. And this blessing was in the nourishment of the first man before he sinned. And this power is destined to be strengthened in man in the time to come, and it will return to its original manner; and even the wild animals will have their nature changed and will eat herbage like domesticated beasts, as it is written (Isaiah 11:7): ״ופרה ודוב תרעינה ואריה כבקר יאכל תבן״. And this verse alludes to this matter, for it uses the future tense: ״לכם יהיה לאכלה״, when it should have said ״לכם הוא לאכלה״ or ״לכם נתתי לאכלה״. Rather, the matter is that it opened in the past tense to indicate the past, and concluded with the future to allude to the future: for the Holy One, blessed be He, will strengthen in that time the transformative power that is in man, until it transforms the food and digests it, and no waste or dregs will remain from it; rather, it will restore it to the form of the substance that departs from the body, and in its measure, no less. עד כאן." "<b>The second blessing</b> is the commandment of reproduction, and that is what it says: ויאמר להם אלהים פרו ורבו. And in it the name Elohim is mentioned, for through this man partners with Elohim, in the manner of what is written (Genesis 4:1): ״קניתי איש את ה׳״, and our Rabbis of blessed memory expounded: three partners share in man — the Holy One, blessed be He, his father, and his mother. And the reason for the commandment is that procreation be a preparation to receive the rational soul, in order to know one's Creator and to serve His service; for if not for this, man would be no different from the beast. And so the prophet said: ״וגם את בניהם אשר ילדו לי״ — that is, to My name; and they expounded it, that Israel are born to His name. And this is the intent regarding them, not like the intent of the wicked, which is merely to settle the world and to eat and drink like beasts; rather, the intent should be for His name alone. And that is that he intend in his begetting toward the purpose of receiving the rational soul and drawing down the holy spirit (ruaḥ ha-kodesh), in the manner of the animal sacrifices, whose intent was the indwelling of the Shekhinah and the drawing down of the holy spirit (ruaḥ ha-kodesh)." "<b>And behold,</b> in these two verses the Holy One, blessed be He, assigned nourishment for all His creatures, from man to beast to the bird of the sky. And it was decreed upon all living creatures that their food be every green herbage — not fruits and seeds; and upon man it was decreed that his food be fruits and seeds, and not the flesh of living creatures, for He did not wish to permit him the flesh of the moving soul (נפש התנועה). And the reason He did not permit him the eating of flesh is that the moving soul has a certain virtue by which it resembles the rational soul (הנפש המשכלת), and it possesses choice regarding its welfare and its sustenance, and it flees from suffering and from death. And when the possessors of the moving soul sinned in the generation of the Flood and corrupted their way, as it is written: ״כי השחית כל בשר״ etc., and destruction was decreed upon them, as it is written (Genesis 6:13): ״קץ כל בשר בא לפני״ — with the exception of Noah the righteous, who was saved from them for the perpetuation of the species — at that time they were permitted to him for slaughter and eating, since their very existence was only for his sake. Even so, He did not give them license over the soul (נפש). Therefore He forbade them a limb from a living animal (אבר מן החי), for He did not permit the soul, only the body. And He also forbade the blood, because it is the sustenance of the soul, as it is stated (Deuteronomy 12:23): ״כי הדם הוא הנפש״. And the Ramban, of blessed memory, wrote that this is the reason for ritual slaughter (sheḥitah), and what our Rabbis of blessed memory said: ״צער בעלי חיים דאורייתא״ — the suffering of living creatures is prohibited by Torah law. And from this we recite the blessing: ״אשר קדשנו במצותיו וצונו על השחיטה״."], :book :genesis, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 1, :notes {0 "Abbreviations resolved: הש\"י = השם יתברך (rendered 'the Holy One, blessed be He' per the standard epithet mapping); עוה\"ז = עולם הזה; רה\"ק and רוה\"ק in comment [3] resolved below.", 1 "Source reads 'ס׳ המגלה' — apparently the same 'book Ha-Megillah' cited elsewhere by Rabbeinu Bahya; identity of the author left unspecified as in the Hebrew.", 2 "The trailing abbreviation ע\"כ (= עד כאן, 'thus far') appears to close a quotation from ספר המגלה or from the Ramban; rendered literally as 'עד כאן' to preserve the boundary marker.", 3 "Abbreviations resolved: רז\"ל = רבותינו זכרונם לברכה (rendered 'our Rabbis of blessed memory'); רה\"ק = רוח הקודש; רוה\"ק = רוח הקודש — both rendered 'the holy spirit (ruaḥ ha-kodesh)'; the distinction between the two abbreviations in the Hebrew is preserved as two occurrences.", 4 "Abbreviations resolved: רז\"ל as in comment [3]; ג\"כ = וגם כן folded into 'And He also'."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T23:28:52.682285530Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}